VirtualObject: destroy the child tree iteratively, so its depth is not the call stack.

The other half of patch `0038`. `children` is a `std::vector<std::shared_ptr<VirtualObject>>`
and the destructor was `= default`, so the compiler tears a tree down by
recursing once per level -- and the depth of a tree is the file's own nesting,
which nothing bounds. With `0038` applied and nothing else, a world with a
60,000-level chain of VObs (9 MB, spliced into `zenkit-node`'s 1.4 KB fixture)
**loads and prints LOADED, and the process then dies with 0xC00000FD** on the
way out. A stack overflow is not catchable, so this is the same class of failure
`0038` removes from the read, one step later, and it is worse in the editor for
the same reason: worlds are unloaded on a `worker_threads` worker, whose default
stack is 4 MB against node's 8 MB main thread.

The destructor walks an explicit stack instead: it moves a child's own children
out and lets the child die childless, so no `shared_ptr` release ever nests. A
child whose `use_count()` is not 1 is left whole -- something else owns it, and
its children are not ours to move.

The declaration moves out of the header because the body needs a definition of
the class it is destroying.

Upstreamable as-is: no API change beyond the destructor no longer being
implicit, no behaviour change on any valid world.

--- a/include/zenkit/vobs/VirtualObject.hh
+++ b/include/zenkit/vobs/VirtualObject.hh
@@ -322,8 +322,11 @@
 		/// \brief Move-constructor.
 		VirtualObject(VirtualObject&&) = default;
 
-		/// \brief Default virtual destructor.
-		~VirtualObject() override = default;
+		/// \brief Virtual destructor. Tears the child tree down iteratively.
+		///
+		/// The depth of a VOb tree comes off the file, so destroying it must not
+		/// recurse -- see the definition in `VirtualObject.cc`.
+		ZKAPI ~VirtualObject() override;
 
 		/// \brief The type of this VObject.
 		///
--- a/src/vobs/VirtualObject.cc
+++ b/src/vobs/VirtualObject.cc
@@ -74,6 +74,34 @@
 		obj.load(in, version);
 	}
 
+	// `children` is a vector of `shared_ptr<VirtualObject>`, so the compiler's
+	// destructor takes the tree apart by recursing once per level -- and the depth
+	// of a tree comes straight off the file, exactly like the depth
+	// `parse_vob_tree` reads it with. Measured on a 60,000-level chain spliced into
+	// `zenkit-node`'s fixture: the world loads, and the process then dies with
+	// 0xC00000FD, a stack overflow, which no `catch` can turn into an error.
+	//
+	// The walk below uses an explicit stack instead. A child is only opened up when
+	// this tree holds its last reference; one that is shared with another owner is
+	// left whole, because its children are not ours to move.
+	VirtualObject::~VirtualObject() {
+		std::vector<std::shared_ptr<VirtualObject>> pending = std::move(this->children);
+		this->children.clear();
+
+		while (!pending.empty()) {
+			auto child = std::move(pending.back());
+			pending.pop_back();
+
+			if (child.use_count() != 1) continue;
+
+			for (auto& grandchild : child->children) {
+				pending.push_back(std::move(grandchild));
+			}
+
+			child->children.clear();
+		}
+	}
+
